    'Kerblam’s warehouse could automate tasks that humans still perform, but labor law requires a minimum human share of its staff. The rule follows political pressure to preserve employment. This is a direct zero variant: technological replacement is available while society deliberately retains jobs as a route to income and participation. The record covers the documented law and its implementation at the major employer, rather than claiming that every occupation on Kandoka is unnecessary.\n\nCorporate management and human political institutions set the employment framework, while the computer system assigns and monitors workers. That supports a concentrated human-control estimate, with uncertainty about the system’s considerable operational discretion. Scarce jobs and pressure on employees do not fully establish the material rights of people outside work, so guarantees remain unresolved. The ninety-percent robot share is a workplace staffing ratio, not evidence that ninety percent of living people do no useful work. The ending’s restructuring changes the rule and is not silently treated as an already durable second economy.',
